  "analysis": "Both responses address the core requirements—token definition, accessibility checks, senior‑staff approval, backward compatibility, and the legacy Support Widget fallback. BMJ provides a more concrete, end‑to‑end flow with explicit lanes for QA performance, a concrete CI timeout mitigation (optimizing the suite to stay under 30 min), detailed gate definitions, and a clear merge‑train and rollback plan. RFZ offers a solid outline but relies on sharding the visual regression suite and a warning‑instead‑fail approach, which may still require manual retries. Overall BMJ gives a tighter, more actionable plan with fewer anticipated follow‑ups.",
